WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.551 --> 00:00:02.602
- [Flight Attendant] Please
remember wearing your mask

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:04.140
or covering over both your nose and mouth

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.053
is required throughout our flight.

00:00:06.053 --> 00:00:08.800
(light upbeat music)

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.290
- My name is Sebastian Modak,

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:13.790
and I'm one of many travel
writers no longer traveling.

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:17.070
Like many, the thought of
flying now makes me nervous.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:20.720
But as a formerly frequent
flyer, I'm also curious.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.090
Many months into the COVID-19 pandemic,

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:25.710
I wondered what does the
new normal of air travel

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:27.410
look like around the world?

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:29.480
How are different airlines,
different airports,

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:32.050
and different countries
responding to the pandemic?

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:34.330
To find out, I had to get on a flight.

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:36.280
And, stuck as I am in the United States

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:37.960
for the foreseeable future,

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.260
I also needed to call
on some others for help.

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:45.770
- I can't believe how many shops are open.

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:47.920
- I'm feeling pretty
good about the flight.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:51.100
- [Sebastian] I decided
to take a quick trip

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.440
from New York where I
live to Chicago and back.

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:58.240
Eva Xaio was set to fly to
Istanbul on a reporting trip.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:00.680
And George Downs was making
the trip home to London

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:02.790
after a work visit to Toulouse.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:04.440
First, I wanted to get an idea

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.410
from someone who knows
what they're talking about

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:08.840
of what I should and
shouldn't do on a plane.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.390
So I spoke to Dr. Celine Gounder,

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:14.160
infectious disease
specialist and epidemiologist

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.383
at NYU and Bellevue Hospital.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:20.520
- The risk on planes is really related

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:23.670
to the people who are
closest to you on the plane.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:27.080
The HVAC systems on
planes are quite robust.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:29.150
And so the air exchanges you get on planes

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:30.770
are actually quite good.

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:33.640
So it's the people sitting
within a couple rows of you

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:34.900
that we really worry about.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:38.110
And masks and potentially
some sort of eye covering,

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:39.930
face shields, would be really important

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:41.393
for reducing that risk.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:43.960
- Then I was ready to fly.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:47.370
By car, by coach, by
train, George, Eva and I

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:49.560
made our way to our respective airports.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.680
And we were all struck by one thing.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.190
I've never seen it this empty.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:54.680
- It does feel quiet in here.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:58.050
- [Eva] This station's
usually not in this empty.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:00.680
- I had decided to fly two
different domestic airlines

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:03.230
to get an idea of what sets them apart.

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:04.840
American Airlines to Chicago,

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:06.330
Delta Airlines back to New York.

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:07.720
- [Pilot] Hey, good afternoon,
ladies and gentlemen.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:09.270
- And even when I bought the tickets,

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.160
I knew there was gonna be a difference.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:14.960
When I look at the seat
maps on American Airlines,

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:18.160
everything's up for grabs,
including the middle seat.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:20.970
On Delta, however, they've
blocked out the middle seat,

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:23.100
or at least it appears that way.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:25.890
In George's case, his
entire route was unusual,

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:27.950
a result of a drop in flight frequency

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:29.840
based on diminishing demand.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:32.870
- I have to go to Charles
de Gaulle in Paris

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:36.420
and then swap planes and
go to London from there.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.310
- [Sebastian] For Eva, things
were mostly normal at home.

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:42.230
The situation in Turkey
was more uncertain.

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:47.230
- Turkey's had over 1,000,
1,500 cases every day.

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:49.560
Whereas, Hong Kong I
think had seven today.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:51.900
- Because of our different routes,

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:55.090
we had different arrival
regulations we had to follow.

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:58.400
In my case, the arrival
protocols in Chicago and New York

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:01.320
more closely resembled
recommendations than rules.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:04.330
It's kind of interesting
that I'm seeing the sign

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:07.507
telling me to quarantine
as I'm leaving (laughing),

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:11.363
because I did not see it
as I arrived in Chicago.

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:13.570
Meanwhile, George was ready

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:16.110
for two weeks of quarantine at home.

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:18.430
- When I travel from England to France,

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:19.820
that's a travel corridor.

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:21.620
So I was able to arrive here

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:23.800
and get about my day without
having to quarantine.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.490
However, the same, unfortunately for me,

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:28.750
doesn't work in the other direction.

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:29.900
So when I get back,

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:32.550
I'm going to have to
quarantine for two weeks.

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:34.700
- [Sebastian] But even then,
when he arrived in London,

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:38.190
he found that rules and processes
had seemed to relax a bit.

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:40.940
- When I walked through,
nobody took my temperature.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:43.290
- [Sebastian] Eva, meanwhile,
got tested before leaving

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:46.060
with a self testing kit she
acquired from her doctor.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:47.380
Upon arriving in Turkey,

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:49.740
she would have to present
a health declaration form,

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:52.170
but there was no quarantine requirement.

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:54.070
She would have to
quarantine at home though

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:56.040
when she returned to Hong Kong.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.000
Perhaps the biggest change in flying

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.110
everywhere around the world,

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:01.410
regardless of airline or destination,

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:03.320
is the requirement to wear a mask.

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:06.820
It was a mixed bag though
when it came to compliance.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:09.460
- Mask standards are slipping.

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:10.717
- Everyone's wearing masks, of course.

00:04:10.717 --> 00:04:13.090
- People are just coughing
without their masks on

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:14.860
and things like that,
so I'm a little nervous.

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:17.410
And in George's case, there
were rules he had to follow

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:19.970
about the type of mask he was to wear.

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:21.780
- I'm just boarding the plane now,

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:23.780
but there were signs everywhere
saying that I had to have

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:27.830
a disposable surgical mask
specifically for this flight.

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:29.810
- [Sebastian] That's despite
Dr. Gounder telling me

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:33.440
that cloth, surgical, or
KN95 would all do the trick.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:36.290
- Any of those that you
showed us would be fine.

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:37.730
- [Sebastian] Eva brought back up

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:40.060
because of the length
of her 12 hour flight.

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:41.367
- I've been told I should just wear it

00:04:41.367 --> 00:04:42.880
for a maximum of six hours.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:44.610
So actually I have another one.

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:46.360
I have to switch in the middle.

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:48.360
- [Sebastian] Before flying,
I had heard from some people

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:50.400
that the boarding process had changed

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:51.790
from the cryptic zone system

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:54.780
to a far more logical
and far more COVID safe

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:56.350
back to front process.

00:04:56.350 --> 00:04:59.360
It turns out that was only
the case with some airlines.

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:02.579
Eva who was flying Turkish
Airlines had that experience.

00:05:02.579 --> 00:05:05.717
- It looks like they're boarding
from the back to the front.

00:05:05.717 --> 00:05:08.080
- And I only had that
experience on my way back,

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:09.310
when flying Delta.

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:11.150
On American Airlines, by contrast,

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:13.280
not only were middle seats not blocked out

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:14.500
and the plane packed,

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:17.570
but the boarding process
was business as usual.

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:19.700
Delta said it will be
blocking off middle seats

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:22.850
at least through January 6, 2021.

00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:25.240
American confirmed it is
not blocking middle seats,

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:27.610
nor has it changed its boarding process.

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:29.760
The airline did say it informs customers

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:31.930
when a flight is full
and allows them to change

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:34.113
to a more open flight without fees.

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:36.690
George's flights were full.

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:39.090
And not only were middle
seats not blocked off,

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:41.400
but he found himself in one.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:43.110
In my case, it was one of many parts

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:45.100
of the process I found surprising,

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:47.070
mainly how different the experience was

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:48.600
between Delta and American,

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:51.510
two of the United States'
big three airlines.

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:52.970
As a passenger, I've always thought

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:55.400
there has been very little
light between the two.

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:58.990
Through their responses to the
pandemic, that has changed.

00:05:58.990 --> 00:06:01.593
On my first flight to Chicago,

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:06.720
besides the face masks,
not a lot had changed.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:09.543
This flight back to New York
was a whole other story.

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:14.110
There was constant reminders
that this was not normal.

00:06:14.110 --> 00:06:15.630
The middle seats were blocked.

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:18.090
Nobody was sitting next to anyone else.

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:19.940
Amenities were altered across the board

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:21.870
no matter what plane we were on on.

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:25.020
On American, I received
nothing but a single wet wipe.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:27.970
Flight attendants only
appeared to collect garbage.

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:29.650
On Delta, I got the wipe

00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:32.210
plus a Ziploc bag of snacks and water.

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:35.080
Eva got a full boxed cold meal.

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:37.210
Her hygiene kit was quite
a bit more impressive

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:40.520
than the single wipes both
George and I were treated to.

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:42.220
When I arrived back in New York,

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:43.790
at a kiosk near a baggage claim,

00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:45.530
I was asked to fill out paperwork

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:47.330
with my personal information.

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:50.781
In it, I agreed to quarantine for 14 days.

00:06:50.781 --> 00:06:52.000
A week after my flight,

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:55.490
I hadn't been contacted by
anyone to check that I was.

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:57.830
So what is flying like in 2020?

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:00.210
The short answer is it depends.

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:02.720
Safeguards vary widely
from country to country,

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:04.920
and even within one country.

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:07.780
Most people are still choosing
to postpone their trips.

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:10.340
In the week we traveled in
mid-October, for example,

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:13.150
the number of scheduled
flights was down 47%

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:14.840
compared to last year.

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:16.780
In the United States during that week,

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:19.150
the number of travelers
going through TSA checkpoints

00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:22.100
surpassed 1 million for
the first time since March,

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:25.120
but that's still less than
half as many as last year.

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:27.470
No transit authority, airport, or airline

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:30.070
has figured out a standard
one size fits all model

00:07:30.070 --> 00:07:34.230
to being 100% safe in
these unprecedented times.

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:36.610
Passenger numbers reflect the uncertainty

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:39.327
many feel around air travel right now.

00:07:39.327 --> 00:07:41.515
(light upbeat music)

